WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.560
So you pick a particular task. The task is not an abstract description

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.680
like a data analysis, but a particular problem or

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.620
activity you give to somebody to solve like in men's golf.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.180
So this is important when you do your cognitive task analysis. You can't just think

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.680
at this level, oh, I'm interested in language learning. Or I'm

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.900
interested in learning second language vocabulary. You have to have a specific

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.260
task. How do you say my favorite teacher was

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.440
great at lecturing in Chinese. That's a task in second language.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.680
Or a task in soft skill domain is how do

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.180
you deal with an angry customer who's calling in to customer

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.360
support. That's a little bit abstract. More particular would

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.240
be to specify what the caller says in there.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.760
So the first step is to describe the features of the initial

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.000
state and the goal state for tasks like this. So think

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.340
about whether you can do that for the men's

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:09.480
golf thing described in the previous slide. What are the features of the initial

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.780
task? And what are the goal states? What's the

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.180
goal state? What does it look like when you're done? So did

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.480
you come up with something like this? The initial state has a data table. It has

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.760
some description of that data and it has a question, you know, and the goal state

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.760
has some analysis, interpretation, and some conclusions.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.860
So then the next question is think about an intermediate state of performing the

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.220
task. A subgoal that unpacks the question mark up here could be something

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:44.460
written on paper or a mental state, a state of thinking, which is

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.940
between the initial and the final goal. This is about the

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.340
task change. The initial state is what the task looks like. The

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.840
goal state is what the solution looks like. Problem solution.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.800
I've had students get confused here and think the initial state is

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.220
knowing how to do, not knowing how to do exploratory data analysis, and

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.400
the final goal state is knowing how to do it. That's not a task

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.760
analysis. That's like a learning progression. So want to get at the task.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.160
So what's something intermediate? What would you do? How would you start?

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:25.400
So Lovett hypothesizes theoretically that there's going to be some

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.700
analysis of the data that's relevant to the question. Maybe not all of

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:34.160
those columns in that data set are relevant. Then you can

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.340
pick one of these other question marks and try to unpack

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.840
it. If you have analysis of the data relevant, what might you do

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.340
next to get towards the goal state? or what might you do before it?

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.480
I can identify the type of variables involved.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:56.340
This is Lovett’s progressive refinement

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.660
of theoretical prescriptive cognitive

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.000
task analysis. Theoretical because we're thinking through it, we're

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.260
trying to determine without data what is likely to be

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.680
the thought process is the cognitive processes for doing tasks like this. It's

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.080
prescriptive because we're trying to do the correct thought processes that

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.260
an expert might engage in. You would continue to unpack or break down the question

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.760
marks by adding further states or sub goals until the thinking between them can

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.160
be performed by entering students. So they want

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.120
each step to break down to levels at which it's going

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.440
to be reasonable to expect a novice student to do

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.460
it. So now change the orientation here, but this is the

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:47.840
seven sub goals or sub tasks in Clark's language. That

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.160
Lovett came up with. Achieving each requires some

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.220
cognitive processes. If then productions can be used to describe

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.500
how each of these is completed. So these are the major sub tasks. The

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.900
productions would be the knowledge components for achieving these subtasks. Lovett

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.300
was inspired by the ACT-R theory, which uses production rules. If then

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.680
production rules to describe procedural knowledge. Notice that they’re

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.620
written in English, but the theory is that they're tacit, they're part of their

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:19.920
cognitive process in the head.
